    <main class="chapter-main" id="main-content">
      <header class="hero hero--experts">
        <span class="hero__chapter">Agentic Horizon · Lesson 13 · Tactic: Build Agents That Learn</span>
        <h1>Agent Experts</h1>
        <p class="hero__dek">Stop deploying agents that execute and forget. Give a narrow agent a self-maintained mental model, close the Act → Learn → Reuse loop, and make useful work improve the next run.</p>
        <ul class="hero__meta" aria-label="Chapter details">
          <li>Video spine · 62:29</li>
          <li>Core lab · 60–90 min</li>
          <li>Course repo · agent-experts</li>
          <li>12 verified 1920×1080 frames</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="hero__promise"><strong>Mastery promise.</strong> By the end, you will be able to distinguish an expert from an ordinary agent or a meta-agentic builder, trace the complete learning loop through real course files, treat expertise as a fallible mental model rather than truth, improve the learning mechanism without hand-editing its output, and defend where an expert is worth the risk.</p>

        <div class="hero-loop" role="img" aria-label="An agent expert takes a useful action, learns by updating a mental model, and reuses that model after validating it against the code">
          <div class="hero-loop__step hero-loop__step--act"><b>Act</b><span>Take one useful, domain-changing action against the real system.</span></div>
          <div class="hero-loop__step hero-loop__step--learn"><b>Learn</b><span>Run a self-improve rubric that updates an isolated expertise file.</span></div>
          <div class="hero-loop__step hero-loop__step--reuse"><b>Reuse</b><span>Load that mental model first, revalidate it against code, then answer or act.</span></div>
        </div>
      </header>

      <div class="mobile-toc">
        <details>
          <summary>Chapter contents</summary>
          <ol>
            <li><a href="#orientation">Orientation</a></li><li><a href="#agents-forget">Agents forget</a></li><li><a href="#expert-definition">The verb: learns</a></li><li><a href="#mental-model">Mental model, not truth</a></li><li><a href="#meta-prompt">The mechanism</a></li><li><a href="#meta-agentics">Meta-agentics</a></li><li><a href="#act-learn-reuse">Act · Learn · Reuse</a></li><li><a href="#expertise-anatomy">Expertise anatomy</a></li><li><a href="#self-improve">The Learn mechanism</a></li><li><a href="#partial-complete">Partial vs complete</a></li><li><a href="#context-delegation">Delegate context</a></li><li><a href="#product-experts">Product experts</a></li><li><a href="#when-to-use">When to build</a></li><li><a href="#current-practice">Current practice</a></li><li><a href="#lab">Graded lab</a></li><li><a href="#mastery">Cumulative mastery</a></li><li><a href="#glossary">Glossary</a></li>
