      <section class="chapter-section" id="orientation">
        <span class="section-number">00</span>
        <h2 class="section-title">Begin with the cold start</h2>
        <p class="section-lead">Imagine hiring the same specialist one thousand times, but erasing their working knowledge after every shift. They may be brilliant. They may have excellent tools. They may even solve each request. Yet the organization does not become more capable because the specialist carries nothing useful into the next run.</p>

        <p>That is the structural failure this lesson names. Context engineering can give an agent a better starting packet. Agentic prompt engineering can give it a better procedure. Sub-agents can isolate purposes. Skills can package repeatable work. None of those moves, by itself, causes the system to learn from the useful work it just performed. If the next run begins from the same starting state, execution scales but expertise does not compound.</p>
        <p>Dan calls this the most controversial lesson of Agentic Horizon because the repair asks you to accept an unusual artifact: a file the agent writes and maintains as its own topic-specific mental model. The file is not documentation. It is not the source of truth. It may be incomplete or wrong. The agent loads it as a fast index, checks it against the code that actually runs, performs useful work, and then updates the index through a controlled self-improve prompt. The value lives in the operating loop, not in pretending the file is perfect.</p>

        <aside class="callout callout--tactic">
          <p class="callout__label">The chapter in one sentence</p>
          <p>An agent expert is an ordinary narrow agent wired into <strong>Act → Learn → Reuse</strong> over a persistent expertise file that it maintains itself and revalidates against the code.</p>
        </aside>

        <h3>What you are training</h3>
        <ul class="learning-objectives">
          <li><strong>Discriminate</strong>Tell an expert from a large model, a long prompt, a memory file, a Skill, or a meta-agentic artifact.</li>
          <li><strong>Trace</strong>Point to the exact course files that supply Act, Learn, Reuse, orchestration, and the mental model.</li>
          <li><strong>Operate</strong>Run read-only Reuse and self-improving Learn without violating repository rules.</li>
          <li><strong>Govern</strong>Validate expertise against code, inspect learned-state diffs, respect size limits, and expect false expertise.</li>
          <li><strong>Judge</strong>Choose high-value domains for experts and refuse the pattern where nothing evolves or you cannot evaluate it.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>The evidence boundary</h3>
        <p>This chapter follows the final checked-out course tree at <code>agentic-horizon/agent-experts</code>, not only the screen recording. That distinction matters. The recording shows the WebSocket event counter being built; the final repository already contains the counter, its plan, and the learned expertise entries. The lab therefore treats that demo as completed-loop archaeology and grades a fresh seed separately. The recording calls one self-improve prompt 99 lines; the current files are 179 and 190 lines. The chapter reports both honestly and uses the repository counts for operations.</p>

        <div class="prime-directive" role="note" aria-label="Lab prime directive">
          <div class="prime-directive__mark">!</div>
          <div><strong>Never hand-edit a non-empty expertise file.</strong><p>If the mental model is wrong, improve the self-improve rubric and rerun it. Editing <code>expertise.yaml</code> directly repairs one artifact while leaving the learning system broken.</p></div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="agents-forget">
        <span class="section-number">01</span>
        <h2 class="section-title">The problem is structural: agents forget</h2>
        <p class="section-lead">A normal agent can produce an excellent answer and still fail as a learning system. The test is not whether run one succeeds. The test is whether run one changes the starting position of run two.</p>

        <div class="move move--hook">
          <p class="move-label">1 · Hook — run one thousand</p>
          <h3>The same expensive rediscovery, forever</h3>
          <p>Point a fresh agent at billing. It finds the calculation path, notices the migration that changed a revenue field, understands which Pydantic model mirrors the table, and answers correctly. The session ends. Tomorrow, another fresh agent repeats the same search. Nothing about yesterday’s useful investigation is available as a starting model. By run one thousand, you have purchased one thousand rediscoveries.</p>
          <p>This is worse than ordinary forgetfulness because repeated use normally creates organizational advantage. A product records analytics, exposes patterns, and improves an algorithm. A team captures review findings and changes a checklist. A specialist remembers where the sharp edges are. A cold-start agent can execute more work without creating any of that compounding state.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="move move--picture">
          <p class="move-label">2 · Picture — flatline versus climb</p>
          <h3>Usage is not learning</h3>
          <div class="forget-map" role="img" aria-label="A cold-start agent repeats the same capability level while a learning system climbs after each run">
            <article><h3>Execute → forget</h3><p>Every run can be good, but the next run starts from the same generic state.</p><div class="run-line run-line--flat"><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span></div></article>
            <article><h3>Execute → store → reuse</h3><p>Each useful action can refine a reusable mental model for the next execution.</p><div class="run-line run-line--climb"><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span></div></article>
          </div>
          <p>The right-hand path does not promise monotonic truth. A mental model can contain false expertise. The diagram shows a changed starting point, not guaranteed correctness. Correctness still comes from revalidating the model against the live code.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="move move--story">
          <p class="move-label">3 · Story — Dan names the missing capability</p>
          <h3>The opening claim is deliberately plain</h3>
          <p>Between roughly 0:11 and 3:25, the video stays on the conceptual problem rather than a code artifact. Dan contrasts software that gets better through use with agents that start fresh, then compresses the failure into one sentence: <em>“Your agents forget. And that means your agents don’t learn.”</em> The frame plan intentionally leaves this beat unframed; a screenshot of narration would add less than the flatline map above.</p>
          <p>The important word is not <em>forget</em> alone. A clean session can be desirable. Context isolation protects focus. The failure is that the system has no deliberate path for carrying validated, domain-specific learning forward. Forgetting becomes fatal when nothing outside the transient context captures the useful result.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="move move--name">
          <p class="move-label">4 · Name it</p>
          <h3>Structural forgetting</h3>
          <aside class="callout callout--term"><p class="callout__label">Dan’s diagnosis · 0:53</p><p><strong>“Your agents forget. And that means your agents don’t learn.”</strong></p></aside>
          <p>Structural forgetting means the execution architecture discards domain understanding at the session boundary. It is not a complaint that the context window is too small, the prompt is weak, or the model is unintelligent. Those dimensions affect a run. A learning path affects what survives between runs.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="move move--example">
          <p class="move-label">5 · Worked example — repeated database archaeology</p>
          <h3>What the database expert avoids</h3>
          <p>Without expertise, a database question begins with broad search: locate model files, find migrations, infer table relationships, identify query patterns, and reconstruct how shared models reach apps. The course database expert instead begins at <code>.claude/commands/experts/database/expertise.yaml</code>. That file already points toward the six Pydantic/table models, migrations, connection and query patterns, and synchronization mechanism.</p>
          <p>The agent does not stop at the summary. It uses the summary to choose which real files to read, then verifies claims against <code>apps/orchestrator_db/models.py</code>, migrations, and related implementation. Reuse changes the search from “discover the whole domain blindly” to “start from a compact map and test it.” Learn later updates that map when validation finds meaningful drift.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="move move--try">
          <p class="move-label">6 · You try — find your rediscovery tax</p>
          <h3>Name one repeated cold start</h3>
          <ol>
            <li>Choose one course-repository question you have asked an agent more than once.</li>
            <li>List the same files, terms, or relationships the agent must rediscover each time.</li>
            <li>Circle only facts that are narrow to one domain and useful as navigation.</li>
            <li>Write what code would need to be checked before any circled fact could be trusted.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>You have identified an expertise candidate only if the stored map would reduce rediscovery <em>and</em> you can name the ground truth used to validate it.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="move move--fail">
          <p class="move-label">7 · Common fail + fix</p>
          <h3>Failure: buy a larger context window</h3>
          <p><strong>Symptom:</strong> every fresh run receives more raw repository content, but no mechanism records what the previous run found useful. Token volume rises; the cold-start architecture stays unchanged.</p>
          <p><strong>Fix:</strong> separate transient working context from persistent learned state. Store a narrow mental model outside the conversation, load it only when the domain is active, validate it against code, and update it through an explicit Learn step.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="move move--current">
          <p class="move-label">8 · Current practice</p>
          <h3>Built-in memory changes the options, not the design question</h3>
          <aside class="current-practice"><p class="current-practice__label">Current practice · verified July 2026</p><p>Claude Code now distinguishes human-written <code>CLAUDE.md</code> instructions from agent-written auto memory, and loads both at session start. That makes cross-session state easier. It does not make all memory equivalent. Dan’s expertise file is deliberately topic-scoped, loaded on demand by one expert, and governed by a domain-specific validation rubric. Choose always-on instructions, auto memory, a Skill, or detached expertise by scope and lifecycle—not by the generic desire to “give the agent memory.”</p></aside>
        </div>

/* AH-5 inherits the TAC-1 teaching grammar and adds learning-loop visuals. */
@import url("../vol-1-tac/01-hello-agentic-coding.assets.css");

:root {
  --chapter-wide: 68.75rem;
  --act-color: var(--warn);
  --learn-color: var(--ok);
  --reuse-color: var(--accent);
}

body {
  background:
    radial-gradient(circle at 90% 1%, color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent-wash) 76%, transparent) 0 18rem, transparent 40rem),
    linear-gradient(180deg, color-mix(in srgb, var(--bg) 95%, var(--bg-card)) 0, var(--bg) 38rem);
}

.skip-link {
  position: fixed;
  top: .5rem;
  left: .5rem;
  z-index: 120;
  padding: .65rem .85rem;
  border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
  color: var(--ink-inverse);
  background: var(--accent-ink);
  transform: translateY(-180%);
}

.skip-link:focus { transform: none; }

.hero--experts {
  background:
    linear-gradient(128deg, transparent 0 58%, color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent-wash) 88%, transparent) 58% 100%),
    var(--bg-card);
}

.hero--experts::after { display: none; }

.hero-loop,
.forget-map,
.definition-map,
.truth-stack,
.meta-equation,
.factory-map,
.loop-diagram,
.expertise-spine,
.learn-pipeline,
.expert-compare,
.delegation-map,
.product-loop,
.decision-matrix,
.practice-board,
.evidence-strip,
.rubric-wrap {
  width: 100%;
  max-width: min(100%, var(--chapter-wide));
  margin-inline: auto;
}

.hero-loop {
  position: relative;
  z-index: 1;
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
  gap: var(--space-3);
  margin-top: var(--space-8);
}

.hero-loop__step {
  position: relative;
  min-height: 9rem;
  padding: var(--space-5);
  border: var(--hairline);
  border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
  background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--bg-card) 88%, var(--accent-wash));
  box-shadow: var(--shadow-card);
}

.hero-loop__step:not(:last-child)::after {
  position: absolute;
  top: 50%;
  right: -.95rem;
  z-index: 2;
  display: grid;
  place-items: center;
  width: 1.9rem;
  height: 1.9rem;
  border-radius: 50%;
  color: var(--ink-inverse);
  background: var(--accent);
  content: "→";
  font-weight: 800;
  transform: translateY(-50%);
}

.hero-loop__step b { display: block; font: 750 var(--text-lg)/1 var(--font-display); }
.hero-loop__step span { display: block; margin-top: var(--space-3); color: var(--ink-soft); font-size: var(--text-sm); }
.hero-loop__step--act { border-top: .4rem solid var(--act-color); }
.hero-loop__step--learn { border-top: .4rem solid var(--learn-color); }
.hero-loop__step--reuse { border-top: .4rem solid var(--reuse-color); }

.prime-directive {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: auto 1fr;
  gap: var(--space-4);
  align-items: center;
  margin-top: var(--space-6);
  padding: var(--space-5);
  border: 2px solid var(--warn);
  border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
  background: var(--warn-wash);
}

.prime-directive__mark {
  display: grid;
  place-items: center;
  width: 3.5rem;
  height: 3.5rem;
  border-radius: 50%;
  color: var(--ink-inverse);
  background: var(--warn);
  font: 800 var(--text-lg) var(--font-display);
}

.prime-directive strong { display: block; color: var(--ink); font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: var(--text-md); }
.prime-directive p { margin-top: var(--space-1); color: var(--ink-soft); }

.forget-map,
.definition-map,
.expert-compare,
.decision-matrix,
.practice-board {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
  gap: var(--space-4);
}

.forget-map article,
.definition-map article,
.expert-compare article,
.practice-board article {
  padding: clamp(1.25rem, 3vw, 2.2rem);
  border: var(--hairline);
  border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
  background: var(--bg-card);
  box-shadow: var(--shadow-card);
}

.forget-map article:first-child { border-top: .4rem solid var(--ink-mute); }
.forget-map article:last-child,
.definition-map article:last-child { border-top: .4rem solid var(--ok); background: var(--ok-wash); }
.forget-map h3,
.definition-map h3,
.expert-compare h3,
.practice-board h3 { margin: 0; }
.forget-map p,
.definition-map p,
.expert-compare p,
.practice-board p { margin-top: var(--space-3); color: var(--ink-soft); }

.run-line { display: grid; gap: var(--space-2); margin-top: var(--space-4); }
.run-line span { display: block; height: .7rem; border-radius: 999px; background: var(--bg-inset); }
.run-line--flat span { width: 74%; }
.run-line--climb span:nth-child(1) { width: 30%; }
.run-line--climb span:nth-child(2) { width: 50%; }
.run-line--climb span:nth-child(3) { width: 72%; }
.run-line--climb span:nth-child(4) { width: 94%; background: var(--ok); }

.truth-stack {
  display: grid;
  gap: var(--space-3);
  padding: var(--space-5);
  border: 1px solid var(--accent);
  border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
  background: var(--accent-wash);
}

.truth-stack__code,
.truth-stack__model,
.truth-stack__answer {
  padding: var(--space-4);
  border-radius: var(--radius-md);
  background: var(--bg-card);
  box-shadow: var(--shadow-card);
}

.truth-stack__code { border-left: .45rem solid var(--ink); }
.truth-stack__model { border-left: .45rem solid var(--accent); }
.truth-stack__answer { border-left: .45rem solid var(--ok); }
.truth-stack b { color: var(--accent-ink); font-family: var(--font-display); }
.truth-stack span { display: block; margin-top: var(--space-1); color: var(--ink-soft); }
.truth-stack__arrow { color: var(--accent-ink); font: 800 var(--text-lg) var(--font-display); text-align: center; }

.meta-equation,
.factory-map,
.loop-diagram,
.learn-pipeline,
.delegation-map,
.product-loop,
.evidence-strip {
  display: grid;
  gap: var(--space-3);
  padding: var(--space-5);
  border: var(--hairline);
  border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
  background: var(--bg-card);
  box-shadow: var(--shadow-card);
}

.meta-equation { grid-template-columns: repeat(7, auto); align-items: stretch; }
.meta-equation__part,
.factory-map article,
.loop-diagram article,
.learn-pipeline article,
.delegation-map article,
.product-loop article,
.evidence-strip article {
  padding: var(--space-4);
  border-radius: var(--radius-md);
  background: var(--bg-inset);
<!DOCTYPE html>
