+      <section class="chapter-section" id="mastery">
+        <span class="section-number">10</span>
+        <h2 class="section-title">Cumulative mastery</h2>
+        <p class="section-lead">Close the chapter. Answer from memory. Reveal only after committing to an answer, then route every miss back to evidence or a lab checkpoint.</p>
+
+        <div class="mastery-grid">
+          <article><h3>Map</h3><p>Can you place every artifact on the application/agentic split and every capability on the Class/Grade ladder?</p></article>
+          <article><h3>Trace</h3><p>Can you follow a request from CLI or orchestrator through a database record into a detached ADW process?</p></article>
+          <article><h3>Diagnose</h3><p>Can you separate context, model, prompt, tool, deterministic runtime, and orchestration failures?</p></article>
+          <article><h3>Transfer</h3><p>Can you grade your own codebase and propose one specialized next rung with observable value?</p></article>
+        </div>
+
+        <section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="Cumulative mastery retrieval">
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">1. What are the two layers every codebase can now contain?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The application layer that serves users and the agentic layer that teaches and runs agents over it.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">2. What unique relationship defines each Class?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Class 1 is in-loop terminal use; Class 2 runs agents out-loop through triggers and deterministic programs; Class 3 adds an orchestrator that manages agents.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">3. List Class-1 Grades 1–7.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Prime + memory, sub-agents, custom tools, closed loops, templates, prompt chains, agent experts.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">4. What are Class-2 Grades 1 and 2?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Out-loop trigger/runtime fabric, then full AI Developer Workflows.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">5. What are Class-3 Grades 1–3?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The orchestrator agent, orchestrator developer workflows, then the orchestrator running ADWs.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">6. Why must Grade-6 components run individually?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>So a failure can be isolated to one step and each component can be reused, tested, or parallelized independently.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">7. What is the formula behind an ADW’s leverage?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Deterministic code controls transitions and evidence; non-deterministic agents provide reasoning inside bounded steps.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">8. What does the detached runner command look like?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p><code>uv run adws/adw_workflows/adw_&lt;type&gt;.py --adw-id &lt;uuid&gt;</code>, spawned with a new session.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">9. How many orchestrator tools exist, and how are they split?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Ten: eight agent-management tools plus <code>start_adw</code> and <code>check_adw</code>.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">10. What exact tool bridges Class 3 into Class 2?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p><code>start_adw</code>; <code>check_adw</code> then observes the launched workflow.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">11. Why is there no need for a “start adw:” parser?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The orchestrator model interprets natural language and emits a typed management tool call.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">12. What is the codebase singularity?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The threshold where agents can run the codebase better than the engineer or team can alone and earn a central place in delivery.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">13. What are the Core Four?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Context, model, prompt, and tools.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">14. Why is <code>expertise.yaml</code> not a source of truth?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>It is a maintained mental model that must be validated against the current implementation, which remains authoritative.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">15. What does “specialize, don’t genericize” require?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Optimize the assembled layer for one codebase and its real use cases, even if some underlying primitives are reusable.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">16. State the capstone operating loop.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Locate the current rung, name the unique missing element, build it with the Core Four, prove its compute advantage, specialize it, and repeat.</p></details></div>
+        </section>
+
+        <aside class="callout callout--why">
+          <p class="callout__label">Scoring</p>
+          <p><strong>14–16 correct:</strong> teach the chapter and run the lab. <strong>10–13:</strong> revisit the relevant Class and reproduce its code trace. <strong>0–9:</strong> redraw the ladder and complete Lab Parts 1–2 before operating the orchestrator. A memorized noun without a real path or causal explanation earns half credit.</p>
+        </aside>
+
+        <h3>Capstone teach-back</h3>
+        <div class="teach-back">
+          <p>Without notes, teach a colleague for five minutes:</p>
+          <ol>
+            <li>Why a codebase has an application layer and an agentic layer.</li>
+            <li>How the three Classes change who initiates and coordinates work.</li>
+            <li>How the seven Class-1 Grades create the foundation.</li>
+            <li>Why ADWs combine deterministic code with non-deterministic agents.</li>
+            <li>How <code>start_adw</code> bridges the orchestrator to a detached workflow.</li>
+            <li>Why the next step is specialization, not a generic platform.</li>
+          </ol>
+          <p class="fine">Expert pass: your listener can grade a fresh repository and name one specific next artifact without opening this chapter.</p>
+        </div>
+      </section>
+
+      <section class="chapter-section" id="glossary">
+        <span class="section-number">11</span>
+        <h2 class="section-title">Capstone glossary</h2>
+        <p class="section-lead">Use these terms narrowly enough that a repository artifact or runtime behavior can prove each one.</p>
+        <dl class="glossary">
+          <div><dt>Application layer</dt><dd>The inner product surface: databases, frontends, backends, scripts, infrastructure, notebooks, and related applications that directly serve or operate the product.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Agentic layer</dt><dd>The specialized outer ring of instructions, agents, tools, feedback, workflows, experts, triggers, evidence, and orchestration that operates the application.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Class</dt><dd>A maturity category defined by a distinct operating relationship: in-loop, out-loop, or orchestrated.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Grade</dt><dd>A new capability dimension within a Class. Grades stack rather than replacing prior capabilities.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Class 1</dt><dd>In-loop agentic primitives initiated by the engineer in the terminal.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Class 2</dt><dd>Out-loop infrastructure and AI Developer Workflows that can run agents without the engineer attending.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Class 3</dt><dd>An orchestrated layer where a custom lead agent manages other agents and eventually launches ADWs.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Prime</dt><dd>An orientation prompt that loads the codebase map, conventions, and starting context.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Sub-agent</dt><dd>A focused agent definition with a narrow purpose and right-sized context, model, prompt, and tools.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Closed loop</dt><dd>A request → validate → resolve structure where evidence from one action becomes input to the next.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Template</dt><dd>An encoded engineering shape that varies conditionally by task type or complexity rather than relying on a free-form plan every time.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Prompt chain</dt><dd>Several independently runnable prompts or agents composed into a larger agent-driven sequence.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Agent expert</dt><dd>A narrow agent that acts, learns by reconciling its mental model with code, and reuses that maintained expertise in future work.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Expertise file</dt><dd>A compact mental model and working memory, commonly <code>expertise.yaml</code>, validated against code rather than treated as authoritative.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>ACT → LEARN → REUSE</dt><dd>The expert loop: perform domain work, update the mental model from current evidence, then apply that model to later questions and plans.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>PITER framework</dt><dd>The course’s name for the out-loop framework in which external triggers and an agent runtime initiate work without the engineer present. The transcript may mishear it as “Peter.”</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>AI Developer Workflow · ADW</dt><dd>A deterministic program that owns state and transitions around bounded non-deterministic agent steps.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>ADW ID</dt><dd>The durable identifier used to recover workflow input, status, step progress, agents, and events from the database.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Orchestrator agent</dt><dd>A custom lead agent with bounded tools to manage workers, inspect system state, report cost, and launch or check ADWs.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>CRUD for agents</dt><dd>Create, read/list/check, update/command, and delete lifecycle operations applied to agent workers.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Core Four</dt><dd>Context, model, prompt, and tools—the invariant beneath every agentic form factor.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Compute advantage</dt><dd>The useful leverage created when more or better-configured compute produces a more capable, reliable, or trusted engineering outcome.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Specialization</dt><dd>Deliberately fitting the agentic layer to one codebase and its important use cases instead of optimizing the whole system for generic reuse.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Zero-touch engineering</dt><dd>The North Star of pushing repeated workflows toward reliable, observable execution without human relay.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Codebase singularity</dt><dd>The threshold where teams of agents can operate the codebase better than the engineer or team can alone and earn a central role in delivery.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Prioritize Agentics</dt><dd>Tactic 08: build the system that builds the system and treat improvement of the agentic layer as a first-class engineering investment.</dd></div>
+        </dl>
+        <p class="publication-note">Current-practice media: 0 YouTube overlays published. The extract proposes one companion for later verification; the build environment could not verify its identity, so the chapter omits it.</p>
+      </section>
+
+      <footer class="chapter-footer">
+        <p class="eyebrow">Volume II · Agentic Horizon · Capstone</p>
